e, `function (event) { return event.data.status == \"NO_IDENTITY\"; }` limits a subscription to devices without an identity."],[569,"For example, this rule allows reading active certificates issued by a specific CA:"],[2425,"For Gateway, sign-in, pods, dependencies and messaging, see Deployment troubleshooting. For device enrollment, see EST diagnostics."],[914,"For initial mTLS, `client_certificate_settings` contains `validation_cas`, `allow_expired` and `chain_level_validation`. The inspected flow receives one certificate credential; it does not guarantee forwarding every handshake intermediate to the validator. For a direct case, configure the bootstrap’s direct issuer as validation CA and test the actual Gateway path."],[2260,"For intermediates, add `-verify_other intermediates.pem`. Require successful response verification and inspect the status corresponding to `device.crt`, issuer, serial, `thisUpdate` and `nextUpdate`. `Response verify OK` validates the response only: also observe `good`, `revoked` or `unknown`. Do not accept an identity based only on the command’s exit code."],[1213,"For logs, networking, pods or Gateway headers, continue in Deployment troubleshooting. For identities issued, revoked or bound partially, use Renewal and recovery and Devices and identities."],[746,"For metrics, traces and technical logs of the deployment, see Troubleshooting. Audit answers \"who tried to change what\"; observability explains how the system behaved."],[409,"For PKI, check the certificate, the chain, OCSP or CRL with the same type of client production uses."],[91,"For production, use Helm so database, identity, credentials, certificates, storage and upgrades remain explicit."],[1055,"For the administrator"],[2418,"For the device to request its certificate, prepare the DMS and EST client."],[1376,"For the firmware developer, `auto` mode is transparent: the device performs its EST enrollment as usual and, as a side effect, it is provisioned in AWS IoT Core with no