e type, issuer and key but keeps the status, so the CA reappears in the state it had when deleted (normally revoked or expired). A different certificate with the same serial number is rejected with a 400 error, and a certificate that already belongs to an existing CA with a 409 error. To avoid inheriting the previous status, use a certificate with a different serial number and check the result after importing. If the new certificate uses the same key, it shares the SKI with the retained VA role of the deleted CA, whose signer is the old certificate: check that the CRL is generated and, if it is not, use `PUT` to set a signer of the new CA (see CRL)."],[774,"Lamassu keeps the inventory, history and status of the identity."],[1947,"Lamassu persists four X.509 states:"],[54,"Lamassu SaaS offers the platform's capabilities in a managed environment. It is the right option when your team wants to focus on PKI and device identities, not on maintaining the infrastructure that supports them."],[1700,"Lamassu saves the CA’s revocation, then attempts to revoke child CAs and issued certificates with `CessationOfOperation`. Propagation continues recursively through child CAs. This is not a transaction over the whole hierarchy: some resources may have changed while other updates failed."],[342,"Lamassu services and Gateway API resources"],[2138,"Lamassu shows the enabled engines in the console."],[506,"Lamassu uses two authorization paths depending on who implements the protected service. They share principals, policies and grants, but they do not interpret the rules the same way."],[1244,"Lamassu's connector for AWS IoT Core syncs device identities with the AWS platform, handling CA registration, Thing provisioning and certificate lifecycle automatically and event-driven."],[1265,"Lamassu's integration with AWS IoT Core bridges the PKI managed by Lamassu and AWS's connectivity platform. When a device obtains its certificate through the EST enrollment process, the connector ensur